{
  "$schema": "https://app.kilo.ai/config.json",
  // Project-scope Kilo config. Everything here serves one goal: keeping an agent
  // from putting a browser window on the operator's screen.
  //
  // The global config sets `permission.bash: "allow"`, so every command - including
  // `playwright test --headed` and `playwright show-report` - runs with no prompt at
  // all. That is why the headless default in playwright.config.ts kept losing:
  // nothing was stopping a run, and `--headed` is one flag away.
  //
  // `bash` becomes an object here, which replaces the global scalar. ORDER IS THE
  // WHOLE MECHANISM: the last matching rule wins, so the catch-all `"*": "allow"`
  // must come FIRST and every exception after it. A catch-all last would silently
  // re-allow everything above it, which is the opposite of what it looks like.
  //
  // Kilo matches each parsed command in a shell block separately, so a chained
  // `foo && playwright test --headed` is denied by the second command's rule even
  // though the first one is allowed.
  //
  // The pairing is deliberate. e2e/global-setup.ts refuses the run inside Playwright
  // with a message explaining what to run instead; these rules stop it before it
  // starts, and they also cover the three commands that never load the Playwright
  // config at all (`show-report`, `show-trace`, `codegen`/`open`) and the
  // `PLAYWRIGHT_ALLOW_HEADED=1` prefix that the guard cannot distinguish from a
  // human's own signature. The guard alone is bypassable by an agent that sets the
  // variable by hand; this layer is not.
  //
  // `deny` is for the window-opening commands. `ask` is for the two interactive
  // debuggers, where a human may genuinely want them and a wrong guess should reach
  // a prompt rather than a silent block.
  "permission": {
    "bash": {
      "*": "allow",

      // Playwright flags that override `use.headless` after the config is read.
      //
      // `--headed` opens a browser window regardless of config — denied.
      // `--ui` launches Playwright's UI mode which also opens a window — asked so a
      //   human can confirm if they genuinely want it. Using `ask` instead of `deny`
      //   prevents blocking other tools that might use `--ui`.
      // `--debug` launches a Playwright inspector — asked for the same reason.
      //
      // The patterns match the flag regardless of what carries it. For example,
      // `npm run test:e2e -- --ui` — the spelling a model reaches for — contains
      // neither `playwright` nor `test:e2e:ui`, and falls through to the catch-all.
      // e2e/global-setup.ts cannot cover it either: `runUIMode` RETURNS at
      // cli/testActions.js:77, and `globalSetup` is only ever constructed inside
      // `runAllTestsWithConfig` (runner/index.js:6949-6954), so the guard runs after
      // the window is already up. Matching the flag is the only shape that catches
      // the command whatever carries it.
      "*--headed*": "deny",
      "*--ui*": "ask",
      "*--debug*": "ask",

      // The HTML report opens a browser window and then waits forever
      // (runner/index.js:3581-3585), and `playwright.config.ts`'s `open: 'never'` does
      // not survive either of these: `getHtmlReportOptionProcessEnv()` reads them
      // FIRST and its result short-circuits the configured option (runner/index.js:3450,
      // 3511). Both are undocumented. e2e/global-setup.ts refuses the same two names
      // and is the load-bearing layer; these rules arrive first and say why.
      //
      // `--reporter`/`--add-reporter` DO get a rule, and `*--reporter*html*` covers
      // both: `--add-reporter=html` contains `--reporter=html`, and the space-separated
      // spellings match too. It is here because it is cheap and the friendliest refusal,
      // not because the in-process one needed it. The argument for adding it at all is
      // that `kilo.jsonc` is PROJECT-scoped, so it does not apply to a run started from
      // another directory, whereas `globalSetup` loads for every invocation.
      //
      // `PW_TEST_REPORTER` is deliberately NOT listed: it has exactly one use in all of
      // Playwright (runner/index.js:5221), nothing in this repository sets it, and
      // `checkHtmlReporterSelection` already refuses it in-process before any reporter
      // is constructed. A deny rule for an internal variable nothing can reach would be
      // a line that reads like coverage.
      "*PLAYWRIGHT_HTML_OPEN*": "deny",
      "*PW_TEST_HTML_REPORT_OPEN*": "deny",
      "*--reporter*html*": "deny",

      // Never loads playwright.config.ts, so the guard cannot see these. All open a
      // window, and the first two block until it is closed.
      "*show-report*": "deny",
      "*show-trace*": "deny",
      "*playwright codegen*": "deny",
      "*playwright open*": "deny",

      // The same commands by npm script name, which is how an agent spells them.
      "*test:e2e:headed*": "deny",
      "*test:e2e:ui*": "deny",
      "*test:e2e:debug*": "ask",
      "*test:e2e:report*": "deny",

      // The guard's own escape hatch. Denying the variable closes the loop: the
      // only remaining way to get a window is a human typing it in their own
      // terminal. `npm run test:e2e:headed` still works for them - it sets the
      // variable inside package.json, which Kilo never inspects.
      "*PLAYWRIGHT_ALLOW_HEADED*": "deny",

      // `scripts/probe-console.mjs` hardcodes `headless: true` and refuses a
      // `--headed` flag or the variable above, exiting 2 with an explanation. These
      // rules stop the same attempt before the process starts, and they cover the
      // browser a script could launch on its own - which no Playwright-side guard
      // can see, because that guard only runs inside a Playwright process.
      //
      // This is the whole reason the probe is a script and not an MCP server: a
      // `permission.bash` glob matches this command, and an `mcp_*_navigate_page`
      // call matches nothing at all.
      "*probe-console*--headed*": "deny",
      "*PLAYWRIGHT_HEADLESS=false*": "deny",

      // A bare `npx tsc` at this repo's root type-checks ZERO files and exits 0,
      // because the root tsconfig is a solution stub - indistinguishable from a
      // clean typecheck, and something a model that never read a doc will run.
      // `npm run tsc` is the real one and does not match these patterns.
      //
      // The re-allows below matter: build mode is the authoritative check, and
      // ORDER IS THE MECHANISM, so the exception must come AFTER the denial.
      // `scripts/tsc-guard.mjs`, linked to `node_modules/.bin/tsc` by `npm
      // install`, refuses the same invocations at the process itself and is the
      // layer that also covers `tsc` and `./node_modules/.bin/tsc`.
      "*npx tsc*": "deny",
      "*npx tsc -b*": "allow",
      "*npx tsc --build*": "allow",

      // Destroying a worktree. On 2026-10-01 a sweep removed a live chat's
      // worktree with a hard-coded `git worktree remove` / `git branch -D` pair,
      // and `node scripts/dispose-worktree.mjs` is now the path that checks the
      // inventory's `action`, the board's claims and locks, and the session
      // database before it deletes anything.
      //
      // The patterns are scoped to a path containing `kilo/`, which covers both
      // `.kilo/worktrees/<name>` and the salvage worktrees under the temp
      // `kilo/` directory. The documented throwaway recipe
      // (`git worktree remove --force /tmp/verify-main`) does not match, so it
      // keeps working — that worktree was created minutes earlier by the same
      // command and holds nothing but a checkout.
      //
      // `-f` is listed beside `--force` because git accepts both, and a rule that
      // only spelled one of them would be defeated by the other.
      //
      // AND THE REVERSED ORDER, which is the same bug twice. git takes
      // `worktree remove [<options>] <path>` and `<path>` first, so
      // `git worktree remove .kilo/worktrees/alpha --force` is the form a model
      // writes by habit — and it matched NONE of the rules above, which all
      // require the flag before `kilo/`, falling through to the catch-all
      // `"*": "allow"` at the top of this table.
      //
      // SO THE TWO RULES BELOW ARE ORDER-AGNOSTIC, and that is the whole design.
      // Every earlier version of this list spelled the order out, and a
      // demonstrated bypass survived every spelling: `git -C
      // .kilo/worktrees/alpha worktree remove --force .` puts `kilo/` BEFORE
      // `worktree remove`, and the throwaway spelling `cd
      // .kilo/worktrees/alpha && git worktree remove --force .` does too. Both
      // returned `allow`, and the first one actually removed a worktree on a
      // scratch repo. Writing the flag, the path, and the order into the pattern
      // is what made this fragile; a pattern that only requires the two tokens to
      // be present cannot be defeated by rearranging them.
      //
      // NEITHER RULE NAMES A FLAG, which closes the second half: a PLAIN
      // `git worktree remove <path>` was allowed, and that is the spelling the
      // 2026-10-01 incident used. Git only refuses it when the tree is dirty,
      // and the victim was CLEAN. So "no --force" was never a property of this
      // layer — the flag was the only thing being matched, and its absence is
      // what the incident looked like.
      //
      // The documented throwaway recipe, `git worktree remove --force
      // /tmp/verify-main`, does not mention `kilo/` and keeps working. Neither
      // rule touches `npm run dispose`, which reaches git through `execFileSync`
      // rather than through this table: the program is the guard, and this layer
      // is the speed bump in front of it.
      "*worktree remove*kilo*": "deny",
      "*kilo*worktree remove*": "deny",
      "*rm -rf*.kilo*": "deny",
      "*rm -fr*.kilo*": "deny",
      "*rm -r -f*.kilo*": "deny",

      // This permission layer matches command strings and is a speed bump, not a
      // repository-aware guard. Retire worktrees through `npm run dispose`.
      //
      // HANDOFF #215 records these equivalent force-delete spellings. Keep each
      // behind a human prompt just like `git branch -D`.
      "*branch --delete --force*": "ask",
      "*branch -d -f*": "ask",

      // HANDOFF #215 records `update-ref -d refs/heads/<branch>` as an unguarded
      // way to delete a local branch ref, so require a human prompt for it too.
      "*update-ref -d refs/heads/*": "ask",

      // This project-scoped deny also catches the bare command: pruning has no
      // target path to match, yet can discard this repository's kilo worktrees.
      "*worktree prune*": "deny",

      // `-d` refuses an unmerged branch, while `-D` overrides that protection.
      // Keep force-deleting a local branch behind a human prompt.
      "*branch -D*": "ask",
    },

    "external_directory": {
      "/tmp/kilo/**": "allow",
      ".kilo/worktrees/**": "allow",
    },
    "edit": {
      ".kilo/**": "allow",
    },
  },

  // Chrome DevTools, scoped to this project rather than the global config, and
  // headless because of the rule above.
  //
  // WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT `browser_open`: nothing in the 36 Playwright specs measures
  // time or memory. For a Canvas 2D editor that replays history and materializes
  // layers, long tasks during a stroke and heap growth across undo/redo are the one
  // axis with no instrument at all. That is a gap `npm run probe:console` cannot close:
  // it reports what the page logged, not what it cost.
  //
  // WHY A SERVER RATHER THAN A SCRIPT, given that a browser MCP call bypasses every
  // permission rule in this file: `permission.bash` matches shell commands and
  // `global-setup.ts` only runs inside a Playwright process, so neither constrains an
  // `mcp_*` call. That is a real hole and it is why `--headless` is passed here rather
  // than relied upon, and why the flags below are not optional.
  //
  //   --headless               no window, per the rule at the top of this file
  //   --no-performance-crux    stops the server sending a trace URL to a Google
  //                            endpoint, which is the whole reason a performance tool
  //                            needs a flag
  //   --no-usage-statistics    the server collects anonymous usage data by default
  //
  // DELIBERATELY NOT `--slim`: it reduces the server to three tools — navigation,
  // JavaScript execution and screenshots — which is exactly the set that measures
  // nothing. It would have disabled the performance tools this server exists for.
  "mcp": {
    "chrome-devtools": {
      "type": "local",
      "command": [
        "npx",
        "-y",
        "chrome-devtools-mcp@latest",
        "--headless",
        "--no-performance-crux",
        "--no-usage-statistics",
      ],
      "enabled": true,
      "timeout": 60000,
    },
  },
}
